1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,362
These photographs were never meant to be published.

2
00:00:03,412 --> 00:00:10,887
In the spring of 1933, a gang fleeing a police raid left behind rolls of undeveloped film.

3
00:00:10,937 --> 00:00:12,842
The police had the film developed.

4
00:00:15,892 --> 00:00:23,880
And America saw, for the first time, the faces of the two people it was hunting: two young people laughing, posing with guns, holding each other.

5
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:26,382
This is the story of Bonnie and Clyde.

6
00:00:26,432 --> 00:00:30,420
And many of the photographs in this video were taken by them.

7
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:35,566
Clyde Barrow was born in 1909 into a poor farming family in Texas.

8
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:38,142
Bonnie Parker was a year younger.

9
00:00:38,192 --> 00:00:41,204
She did well at school and wrote poetry.

10
00:00:41,254 --> 00:00:44,237
She married at fifteen; the marriage did not last.

11
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:47,240
They met in Dallas in January 1930.

12
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:50,155
Bonnie was nineteen, Clyde was twenty.

13
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:52,761
A few weeks later Clyde was arrested.

14
00:00:52,811 --> 00:00:58,625
Clyde was sent to Eastham prison farm, one of the harshest prisons in Texas.

15
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:01,013
What he lived through there changed him.

16
00:01:01,063 --> 00:01:05,214
To get out of working in the fields, he had two of his toes cut off.

17
00:01:05,264 --> 00:01:08,157
His mother, though, had already applied for his parole.

18
00:01:08,207 --> 00:01:12,571
Clyde was released soon after, in February 1932.

19
00:01:12,621 --> 00:01:18,006
When he walked out, one thing was on his mind: one day he would come back to Eastham.

20
00:01:18,056 --> 00:01:22,897
Unlike the films, Bonnie and Clyde were not great bank robbers.

21
00:01:22,947 --> 00:01:29,901
Most of the time they robbed gas stations and small shops.

22
00:01:29,951 --> 00:01:32,589
Sometimes they got away with only a few dollars.

23
00:01:32,639 --> 00:01:35,346
America was in the middle of the Great Depression.

24
00:01:35,396 --> 00:01:39,092
While banks were taking people's homes, newspapers wrote about this young couple almost as heroes.

25
00:01:39,142 --> 00:01:40,828
But the truth was different.

26
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:46,910
The gang is believed to be responsible for the deaths of at least thirteen people, nine of them police officers.

27
00:01:46,959 --> 00:01:49,164
April 1933.

28
00:01:49,214 --> 00:01:54,002
The gang moved into an apartment above a garage in Joplin, Missouri.

29
00:01:54,051 --> 00:02:01,697
With them were Clyde's older brother Buck, Buck's wife Blanche, and the gang's youngest member, Jones.

30
00:02:01,747 --> 00:02:04,108
The neighbours grew suspicious.

31
00:02:04,158 --> 00:02:07,204
On the thirteenth of April the police came to the door.

32
00:02:07,254 --> 00:02:08,332
Two officers were killed in the shootout and the gang escaped.

33
00:02:08,382 --> 00:02:13,210
They left behind guns, a poem Bonnie had written, and those rolls of film.

34
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,980
Two days later the photographs were on the front page of the newspaper.

35
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:22,258
The pose of Bonnie with a cigar in her mouth and a pistol in her hand spread across the country.

36
00:02:22,308 --> 00:02:24,410
But that picture had been a joke.

37
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:28,896
And that joke made Bonnie the most recognised woman criminal in America.

38
00:02:28,946 --> 00:02:31,095
Fame made escape harder.

39
00:02:31,145 --> 00:02:33,387
Now everyone knew their faces.

40
00:02:33,437 --> 00:02:35,004
They could not stay in hotels.

41
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:37,785
They slept in the car and washed in streams.

42
00:02:37,835 --> 00:02:43,379
In June 1933, in Texas, Clyde missed a warning sign on the road.

43
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:45,701
The car overturned and caught fire.

44
00:02:45,752 --> 00:02:50,187
Bonnie's leg was badly burned.

45
00:02:50,237 --> 00:02:52,762
After that day she could barely walk; most of the time Clyde carried her.

46
00:02:52,812 --> 00:02:56,056
In July they were surrounded at a motel in Missouri.

47
00:02:56,106 --> 00:02:57,834
Buck was shot in the head.

48
00:02:57,884 --> 00:03:00,939
Four days later they were surrounded again in Iowa.

49
00:03:00,989 --> 00:03:05,205
To save Bonnie, Clyde had to leave his brother behind.

50
00:03:05,255 --> 00:03:07,338
Buck died a few days later.

51
00:03:07,388 --> 00:03:08,924
Blanche went to prison.

52
00:03:08,974 --> 00:03:11,118
January 1934.

53
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:13,876
Clyde did the thing he had been waiting years to do.

54
00:03:13,926 --> 00:03:18,567
He raided Eastham prison farm and broke out five inmates.

55
00:03:18,617 --> 00:03:21,709
For Texas, that was the last straw.

56
00:03:21,759 --> 00:03:28,295
The head of the prison system called in Frank Hamer, a former Texas Ranger.

57
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:30,145
Hamer had one job: to find Bonnie and Clyde.

58
00:03:30,195 --> 00:03:33,586
He tracked them for months and noticed a pattern.

59
00:03:33,636 --> 00:03:38,327
Wherever the couple ran, in the end they always came back to see their families.

60
00:03:38,377 --> 00:03:43,170
The family of Henry Methvin, one of the escaped inmates, lived in Louisiana.

61
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:49,719
Hamer made a deal with the Methvin family: clemency for their son in exchange for the road the couple would take.

62
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:52,194
Bonnie knew the end was near.

63
00:03:52,244 --> 00:03:55,603
Weeks before her death she gave her mother a poem.

64
00:03:55,653 --> 00:04:03,951
In it she told their own story and wrote how it would end: one day they would be shot together and buried side by side.

65
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:06,538
The twenty-third of May, 1934.

66
00:04:06,588 --> 00:04:10,614
A forest road near the town of Gibsland, Louisiana.

67
00:04:10,664 --> 00:04:14,381
Six officers had been waiting in the bushes for hours.

68
00:04:14,431 --> 00:04:18,858
By the side of the road, Methvin's father stood with his truck as if it had broken down.

69
00:04:18,908 --> 00:04:22,770
At a quarter past nine in the morning, a Ford V8 approached.

70
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:25,772
Clyde slowed down when he saw the truck he recognised.

71
00:04:25,822 --> 00:04:27,421
The officers opened fire.

72
00:04:27,471 --> 00:04:29,713
It was all over in seconds.

73
00:04:29,763 --> 00:04:34,191
More than a hundred and fifty bullets hit the car.

74
00:04:34,241 --> 00:04:36,130
Bonnie was twenty-three, Clyde was twenty-five.

75
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:39,434
Within hours the news had spread across the country.

76
00:04:39,484 --> 00:04:43,588
Queues formed for days outside two funeral homes in Dallas.

77
00:04:43,638 --> 00:04:47,754
In two days the newspapers sold almost half a million extra copies.

78
00:04:47,804 --> 00:04:51,004
The last wish in Bonnie's poem did not come true.

79
00:04:51,054 --> 00:04:52,940
Her mother would not allow it.

80
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:56,846
Bonnie and Clyde were buried in two separate cemeteries in Dallas.

81
00:04:56,896 --> 00:05:03,596
What remained were those photographs: frames taken as a joke that turned two young people into a legend.

82
00:05:03,646 --> 00:05:08,950
Do you think we would still remember Bonnie and Clyde today if those rolls of film had never been found?

83
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,228
Write it in the comments.
